-- title:
--   λ-adic eigenplane of a weight-two newform in T_λ(J₀(M))
-- statement:
--   Fix $M \ge 1$ and a cusp form $g$ of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the project's sense (a normalised eigenform such that no proper divisor of the level carries a good eigensystem for it), a prime $\lambda$, and a finite set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ with $\lambda \in S$ and containing every prime divisor of $M$. Let $O'$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field in which $\lambda$ lies in the maximal ideal, let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of cusp forms generated by the Hecke generators away from $S$) to $\mathbb{C}$ sending each [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra.T`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L77) at a prime $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$, to the $\ell$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ to $O'$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a ring $O''$ carrying the same list of properties (complete discrete valuation ring, characteristic zero, finite residue field), an $O'$-algebra structure making $O''$ module-finite over $O'$ with local and injective structure map, and a $\mathbb{Z}_\lambda$-algebra structure, such that for every fraction field $K$ of $O''$ the following holds. Equip $J_0(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with the Hecke-algebra action [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleBar M`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L82), and let $T$ be the $\lambda$-adic Tate module, the module of sequences $x : \mathbb{N} \to J_0(M)$ with $x_0 = 0$ and $\lambda \cdot x_{n+1} = x_n$. Then for every $\mathbb{Z}_\lambda$-module structure on $T$ whose scalar multiplication is given level by level by reduction of $a \in \mathbb{Z}_\lambda$ modulo $\lambda^n$, every monoid homomorphism $\rho_M$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to $\mathrm{End}_{O''}(O'' \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\lambda} T)$ acting on pure tensors through the Galois action on $T$, and every ring homomorphism $T_M$ from [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlg`](def/HeckeGalois_EichlerShimura.html#L14) $= \mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ to that endomorphism ring acting on pure tensors through the Hecke action, there is a $K$-submodule $W$ of $K \otimes_{O''} (O'' \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\lambda} T)$ with $\dim_K W = 2$, stable under the base change to $K$ of each $\rho_M(\sigma)$, such that for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid M$ and $\ell \notin S$: the base change of $T_M(X_\ell)$ acts on $W$ as multiplication by the image in $K$ of $\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))$, and for every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit in $A$ and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ acting on the residue field of $A$ by $x \mapsto x^{\ell}$, the trace on $W$ of the restriction of the base-changed $\rho_M(\sigma)$ equals that same scalar. The proof visibly discards the hypotheses that $g$ is a newform, that $\chi_g$ computes the $q$-coefficients of $g$, and that $S$ contains the primes dividing $M$.
--
--   This is the Eichler–Shimura construction of the $\lambda$-adic Galois representation attached to a weight-two newform, realised as a two-dimensional Hecke eigenplane inside $K \otimes T_\lambda(J_0(M))$ on which Frobenius traces at good primes are the Hecke eigenvalues. It is the source of the $\lambda$-adic representations used downstream to produce stable lines, inertia statements and residual representations for the modularity arguments.
